                'The Prophet (PBUH) said: "Whoever reads a letter from the Book of Allah will have a good deed, and each good deed is multiplied by ten. I do not say that Alif-Lam-Mim is one letter, but Alif is a letter, Lam is a letter, and Mim is a letter." (Tirmidhi). Read this hadith slowly. It is a math problem hidden in a sentence.',
                'A single page of the Arabic Quran has roughly 200 letters. According to this hadith, that is 2,000 good deeds per page. A typical juz (one-thirtieth of the Quran) has about 10 pages. That is 20,000 good deeds per juz. The entire Quran is 30 juz, which means a complete reading earns you approximately 600,000 good deeds. And the Ramadan multiplier — every good deed multiplied by 70 or more in that month — pushes these numbers into the millions.',
                'But the Prophet (PBUH) was not just giving us arithmetic. He was teaching us how to think about small things. He specifically said, "I do not say Alif-Lam-Mim is one letter." He wanted us to count each letter separately. Why? Because the human mind dismisses small things. We think, "It is just one letter, just one word, just one page." The Prophet (PBUH) interrupts that whisper. Each letter matters. Each letter is recorded. Nothing is wasted.',
                'This hadith also reshapes our relationship with the Quran as beginners. If you are someone who finds it difficult to recite, who can only read a few lines before you are tired, take heart. The hadith does not say "whoever recites fluently." It says "whoever reads a letter." Even the slow, halting reader, who takes five minutes to get through one short surah, is racking up rewards letter by letter, at the same rate as the fluent reciter.',
                'Notice also that the hadith mentions a multiplied reward. Allah, out of His generosity, does not count one good deed as one. He counts it as ten, and sometimes more. In Ramadan, He multiplies further. On Fridays, He multiplies further. The whole system is rigged in your favour, if only you would show up.',
                'So the next time you think, "I do not have time to read Quran today," remember the math. A single page is two thousand deeds. A single surah, like al-Ikhlas (which takes less than a minute), is several hundred deeds. The question is not whether you have time. The question is whether you are willing to collect what is being handed out for free.'
